	3. Adding Redundancy and Fault tolerance
		a. Single Point of Failure
			§ If the failure of the single system component causes whole system to shut down or causes it to crash, that component is single point of failure
			§ Single point of failure should be avoided as it decreases the  availability of the system.
			§ Clustering and load balancers are ways to mitigate server as single pint of failure and RAID is for data/disk redundancy
				
		b. Disk Redundancies
			i. RAID-0
				§ Striping without parity
				§ High performance, no fault tolerance
			ii. RAID-1
				§ Mirroring
				§ Duplicates data for fault tolerance but requires twice the disk space
			iii. RAID-5
				§ Striping with parity
				§ Fault tolerance, only requires an additional disk for redundancy
				§ Minimum 3 disk, can tolerate 1 disk failure.
			iv. RAID-6
				§ Striping with parity
				§ Fault tolerance, only requires an additional disk for redundancy
				§ Minimum 4 disk, can tolerate 2 disk failure.
			v. RAID-10
				§ Combine RAID -1 and RAID-0 i.e. striping and mirroring
				§ Minimum 4 disk required and can tolerate 2 disk failure
			
			§ NAS (Network Access Storage)
				§ In addition to using external storage devices connected directly to organization network and act as file servers, we can also use NAS which looks like servers but are storage devices
				§ Are often implemented in conjunction with RAID to provide HA
				§ Use proper encryption, authentication and log access
				
			§ SAN (Storage Area Network)
				§ Collection of different NAS 
				§ Designed specifically to perform block storage functions that may consists of NAS devices.
				
		c. Server Redundancy and High Availability
			§ Distributive allocations increases availability hardware, s/w and network
			§ HA ensure component is always available  even if one fails.
			
			i. Failover clusters for High Availability
				§ The failover clusters provide availability.
				§ If the component fails, the backup system takes over and the flow of the process fails back on backup system.
				§ The backup system keeps monitoring main system's heartbeat to check its availability
			ii. Clustering versus Load balancing
				1) Cluster
					§ Logical group of servers acting as one server
				2) Load balancer
					§ Manage load across multiple devices.
					§ Servers can be added and removed based on utilization
					§ Performs constant health checks.
					§ System has many servers, and when load on the system is more, load balancers helps to distribute the load amongst the servers to provide better efficiency
					§ The configuration can be active-passive (at least one system is passive and backup and checking up on other system) or active-active (all systems are actively participating)
					§ Load balancers use scheduling round-robin, source ip affinity mapping techniques to distribute the load
					§ In round-robin technique, the nest request is sent to next server
					§ In source ip affinity mapping, the ip of the source is mapped to a server and all the requests by that user are sent to same server for that session.
					
